    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. Taxation
    lesson(
        module=M_ECON, topic="Government revenue and taxation",
        title="Taxes: direct, indirect and the duty of the taxpayer",
        slug="cit2-taxation-types-and-taxpayer-duties",
        minutes=22,
        summary="Direct and indirect taxes with Cameroonian examples, proportional and progressive taxes, how VAT is added to a price, what makes a tax fair, and the rights and duties of a taxpayer.",
        objectives=[
            "Define a tax and distinguish direct from indirect taxes",
            "Distinguish proportional and progressive taxes",
            "Calculate value added tax on a price",
            "Explain the qualities of a good tax and the duties of a taxpayer",
        ],
        key_concepts=["Tax", "Direct tax", "Indirect tax", "VAT", "Progressive tax", "Tax evasion", "Taxpayer"],
        blocks=[
            H("Introduction"),
            T("When you buy a bag of rice or top up your phone, part of the price may go to the State. When a teacher receives a salary, part may be taken as income tax before it reaches the bank. These payments are taxes.\n\n"
              "Taxes are the main way a State pays for schools, roads and hospitals. In this lesson you learn the types of tax and why paying them honestly is a civic duty."),
            D("A **tax** is a compulsory payment made to the State by people and firms. It is not payment for one particular service you receive.", title="Tax"),
            H("Direct and indirect taxes"),
            KEY("Two main types", "The difference is who really bears the cost.", cards=[
                ["Direct tax", "Paid straight to the tax authority by the person or firm that bears the cost. Examples: personal income tax, company (profits) tax, land and property tax, and local council taxes."],
                ["Indirect tax", "Added to the price of goods and services. The seller collects it and passes it on to the State, but the buyer bears the cost. Examples: value added tax (VAT), excise duties, customs duties on imports."],
            ], note="In Cameroon the tax offices are the General Directorate of Taxation (for domestic taxes) and the General Directorate of Customs (for goods crossing the border)."),
            T("**Value added tax (VAT)** is charged at each stage on the value added to goods and services, and the final buyer bears it. In recent years Cameroon's rate has been stated as 19.25 per cent (a basic rate plus a council surcharge). Rates are changed by the finance law, so check the current rules before quoting a rate in a project."),
            H("Proportional and progressive taxes"),
            T("A **proportional tax** takes the same percentage from everyone, whatever the amount. A **progressive tax** takes a higher percentage from larger incomes. A **regressive tax** takes a larger share of a poor person's income than of a rich person's. Many income taxes are progressive, so that people with higher incomes contribute a higher share."),
            EX("Worked example 1: is the tax progressive?", intro="Ngum earns 100 000 FCFA a month and pays 10 000 FCFA in income tax. Mbarga earns 400 000 FCFA and pays 80 000 FCFA (figures invented for practice). Is the tax proportional or progressive?", steps=[
                S("Find Ngum's rate", math=r"\frac{10\,000}{100\,000} \times 100 = 10\%"),
                S("Find Mbarga's rate", math=r"\frac{80\,000}{400\,000} \times 100 = 20\%"),
                S("Compare", text="The higher income pays a higher percentage, so the tax is **progressive**."),
            ]),
            EX("Worked example 2: adding VAT to a price", intro="A shop in Douala sells a generator for 100 000 FCFA before VAT. If VAT is charged at 19.25%, what is the price the buyer pays? (Rate used for teaching; check the current rate.)", steps=[
                S("Find the tax", math=r"100\,000 \times \frac{19.25}{100} = 19\,250"),
                S("Add to the price", math=r"100\,000 + 19\,250 = 119\,250"),
                S("State the answer", text="The buyer pays **119 250 FCFA**, of which 19 250 FCFA is VAT collected for the State."),
            ], check=r"119\,250 \div 1.1925 = 100\,000"),
            H("What makes a good tax?"),
            T("Economists describe four qualities. A tax should be **fair** (people pay according to their ability), **certain** (people know how much, when and how to pay), **convenient** (easy to pay) and **cheap to collect** (collecting it should not use up much of what it earns). A tax that is unfair or confusing is more likely to be avoided."),
            H("Rights and duties of the taxpayer"),
            T("**Duties:** register with the tax authority, declare income honestly, keep records, pay on time and keep receipts. **Rights:** to be told the rules, to receive a receipt, to be treated with respect and to challenge a tax decision through the proper channels.\n\n"
              "**Tax evasion** means illegally hiding income or refusing to pay what is due. It is a crime. **Tax avoidance** means using the rules to pay as little as the law allows. Evasion hurts everybody: the missing money means fewer schools, clinics and roads."),
            MISTAKE("Calling VAT a direct tax", "VAT is added to the price and paid by the buyer through the seller. It is an indirect tax. Income tax paid straight from salary is direct."),
            MISTAKE("Thinking a progressive tax takes more money in francs only", "Progressive means the percentage (the rate) rises as income rises, not only the amount in francs."),
            MISTAKE("Saying paying no tax helps the poor", "Tax evasion reduces the money for public services that poor families depend on. Where a tax is unfair, the proper response is to ask for the rules to be changed, not to evade."),
            QUIZ("Which of these is an indirect tax?", ["Company profits tax", "Personal income tax", "Value added tax", "Tax on land"], 2,
                 "VAT is added to the price of goods and the final buyer bears it, so it is indirect.",
                 hint="Which tax is added to the price you pay in a shop?",
                 feedback=["Firms pay this directly on their profits.", "Paid directly by the earner.", "", "Paid directly by the owner."]),
            QUIZ("A tax takes 5% from every income, whether small or large. This is:", ["Progressive", "Regressive", "Proportional", "Indirect"], 2,
                 "The same percentage for everyone is a proportional tax.",
                 hint="Look at whether the rate changes as income changes.",
                 feedback=["The rate would have to rise with income.", "The rate would have to fall as income rises.", "", "Direct or indirect is about how the tax is collected, not the rate."]),
            H("Practice questions"),
            PQ("Distinguish between a direct tax and an indirect tax, with one example of each.", "A direct tax is paid straight to the tax authority by the person who bears it, for example income tax. An indirect tax is added to the price of goods and collected by the seller, but borne by the buyer, for example VAT."),
            PQ("Calculate the VAT at 19.25% on a price of 20 000 FCFA before VAT, and the total price. (Rate for teaching; check the current rate.)", "VAT = 20 000 x 19.25 / 100 = 3 850 FCFA. Total = 20 000 + 3 850 = 23 850 FCFA."),
            PQ("Explain tax evasion and give two effects on the community.", "Tax evasion is illegally hiding income or not paying tax that is due. It reduces public revenue, so there is less for schools, health and roads, and it makes honest taxpayers carry more of the cost."),
            PQ("State two duties of a taxpayer.", "Any two of: register with the tax authority, declare income truthfully, keep records and receipts, pay on time."),
            SUM("A tax is a compulsory payment to the State, not payment for a particular service.",
                "Direct taxes (income, company, land) are paid by those who bear them; indirect taxes (VAT, excise, customs) are added to prices.",
                "A proportional tax has a fixed rate; a progressive tax has a rate that rises with income. VAT is added as a percentage of the price. Check the current rate.",
                "Taxpayers must register, declare honestly, keep receipts and pay on time. Evasion is a crime that harms public services."),
        ],
    ),
